    {
      "entity_id": "surya-claim-r1-annealing-baseline-2026-08",
      "evidence_class": "evidence-backed",
      "kind": "claim",
      "local_ref": "c1",
      "op": "entity.declare",
      "title": "R1 annealing baseline (Aug 2026): conventional crystalline Si shows no significant annealing below ~200 C, but ultra-thin defect-engineered SHJ cells (20 um class) fully recover from 1 MeV electron (1e15 e/cm2) and 300 keV proton damage within hours at 65-100 C under illumination - inside the realistic sunlit LEO operating band (+50..+80 C); the effect is strongly thickness-gated (20 um: 120 mV/hr Voc recovery; 80 um: nearly none) and undemonstrated at the 100 um design point"
    },
    {
      "entity_id": "surya-claim-r1-defect-inventory-2026-08",
      "evidence_class": "evidence-backed",
      "kind": "claim",
      "local_ref": "c2",
      "op": "entity.declare",
      "title": "R1 defect inventory (Aug 2026): lifetime damage follows 1/L^2 = 1/L0^2 + K_L*Phi (K_L ~1.9e-10 per 1 MeV electron, n/p 1 ohm-cm); thermal annealing stages are defect-resolved - E-center (V-P) 150-180 C (Ea 0.94 eV), divacancy ~300 C (Ea 1.3 eV, first-order), A-center (V-O) 300-400 C; reverse annealing observed in n+p cells >400 C at fluences >1e14 e/cm2; injection-enhanced annealing of Si defects is established at mechanism level (Lang-Kimerling; Arimura; Makarenko)"
    },
    {
      "entity_id": "surya-claim-r1-thickness-scaling-2026-08",
      "evidence_class": "evidence-backed",
      "kind": "claim",
      "local_ref": "c3",
      "op": "entity.declare",
      "title": "R1 thickness scaling (Aug 2026): the short-base argument is quantified and flight-proven - base width <25 um tolerates lifetimes <250 ns vs ~1 ms conventional (>1000x relaxation, DiNetta 1991); optimum EOL thickness computed as low as 2 um (Landis 1991); 60 um Ga-doped SHJ beats 180 um PERC EOL at every tested fluence at 3x lower mass (Cariou 2023); NASDA flew 50 um cells from 1983 and qualified ~60000 100 um CICs, with thinner cells winning above ~1e15 e/cm2"
    },
    {
      "entity_id": "surya-claim-r1-doping-annealing-2026-08",
      "evidence_class": "evidence-backed",
      "kind": "claim",
      "local_ref": "c4",
      "op": "entity.declare",
      "title": "R1 doping and annealing (Aug 2026): Ga-doped p-type SHJ is the radiation-hard and annealing-compatible choice - Cz p(B) ultra-thin cells show ~90% Voc recovery at 100 C under light while Cz n(P) shows none; classic n-on-p polarity wins on radiation (proton damage coefficient >=2x worse for p-on-n); BO-LID is a distinct injection-activated mechanism absent in n-type and Ga-doped material; the program's 100 um n-type design-point assumption is challenged by this evidence"
    },
    {
      "entity_id": "surya-claim-r1-gap-shape-2026-08",
      "evidence_class": "evidence-backed",
      "kind": "claim",
      "local_ref": "c5",
      "op": "entity.declare",
      "title": "R1 gap shape (Aug 2026): four named gaps - (i) no published thickness-resolved annealing rates at 80-100 um (the design point sits between 20 um full-recovery and 80 um near-zero data); (ii) mechanism of thin-cell low-temperature annealing unresolved (injection-enhanced bulk vs surface proximity/gettering; partly proprietary); (iii) no peer-reviewed in-orbit annealing flight dataset for Si (documented for GaAs HS 393 only); (iv) no combined radiation + illumination + thermal-cycling annealing campaign at realistic LEO profile"
    },
